<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Pins the second way a z-icon comes out blank, which the resolved font stack cannot explain.
     Both spans carry the SAME class and so request the same ::before glyph, and both resolve the
     same stack "ZK85Icons, FontAwesome": a declared webfont either way, so a check that asks only
     which family resolved passes both and reports neither. Only the weight differs.

     ZK's base rule is [class*="z-icon"]{font: normal normal normal 14px/1 FontAwesome}, a
     shorthand that resets font-weight to 400, and ZK declares family FontAwesome at both 900
     (fa-solid-900) and 400 (fa-regular-400). Font Awesome 6 keeps most glyphs in the solid face
     only, so weight 400 selects the regular face and money-bill-wave is simply not in it. Weight
     900 selects the solid face and it draws.

     Measured on a clean-room page that shipped with five blank icons and a clean LAYOUT: block.
     The second span is the negative control: a rule that reports both has gone back to firing on
     the class, and a rule that reports neither has been switched off. -->
<zk>
	<style>
		.iw-page { padding: 20px; font-size: 32px; }
		.iw-solid { font-weight: 900; }
	</style>
	<vlayout spacing="12" sclass="iw-page">
		<span sclass="z-icon-money-bill-wave iw-blank"/>
		<span sclass="z-icon-money-bill-wave iw-solid"/>
	</vlayout>
</zk>
